@@yThe barracks compound is a functional rectangle of whitewashed stone-and-timber construction, oriented to catch the prevailing wind through louvered high windows that double as archer-slots if the compound needs defending.
@@bPatrol schedules, transit-seal rotation lists, and a hand-drawn map of the current cairn checkpoint network cover the duty board beside the entrance — the cartographic obsession of an institution that defines itself through documented territory.
@@yA rack of ironwood staves stands by the inner wall, and a senior Warden officer marks the patrol ledger at a field desk in the courtyard with the focused attention of someone who regards paperwork as the actual work.
@@bStairs at the compound's rear corner lead up to the Cairn Tablet Reading Room, where Warden scholars maintain transcription copies of the area's legal infrastructure archive.@@N
