@@ySalt Mirror Crossing@@N~
@@yThe Salt Mirror Crossing is the most dangerous section of the northern route in clear daylight — a stretch of flats where the salt surface is thinnest and most reflective, concentrating light from above and below into a column of brilliance that can cause progressive corneal damage within hours without proper eye protection.
@@bCaravans cross this section at dawn and dusk using the same shadow-timing logic that once governed the obelisk travel windows in the Eastern Desert — the oblique light angles of dawn and dusk reduce the reflection intensity to survivable levels while the direct overhead sun makes midday crossing medically inadvisable.
@@yThe route through the crossing is marked by a line of black-painted stones placed by the guides specifically because black is the one color that does not disappear into the reflective background, visible at distance even in the worst light conditions.
@@bOn each stone a guide notation is scratched: bearing marks, footing quality assessments, and the occasional route song couplet reminding travelers of the timing rule — dawn is safe, dusk is safe, midday is the Flats' way of claiming travelers for the salt.
@@yThe crossing feels suspended in time, the white surface and doubled horizon creating a sensation of being between worlds rather than moving through a specific place.@@N
