@@ySalt Cricket Breeding Ground@@N~
@@yA shallow depression north of the Crystal Ridge serves as the Mirror Flats' primary salt cricket breeding ground — a seasonal aggregation point where the large, pale insects swarm in the hundreds during the brief moisture events when coastal fog reaches inland far enough to trigger their reproductive cycles.
@@bThe breeding ground's floor is riddled with the crickets' characteristic mineral-gnaw excavations — small circular feeding pits where the insects extract soluble minerals from the salt crust surface in a dietary process that damages the crust enough to weaken its load-bearing capacity in the area immediately around each colony.
@@yThe crickets are individually harmless, but a Fog Season swarm reaching breeding ground density can panic draft animals, coat travelers in mineral dust, and create enough shell-crunch noise to mask the hollow-ring sound of worm tunnel collapse testing — three hazards combined into one apparently benign encounter.@@N
