@@yShattered Evaporation Basin@@N~
@@yA section of the Mirror Flats exposes the geological record of the ancient Iseth Reach river's terminal evaporation — a collapsed section of salt crust that reveals deep mineral layering below, the stratified record of the river's final seasonal flooding cycles before it dried entirely.
@@bThe Great Oasis Measuring House's scholars have counted at least forty distinct seasonal layers in core samples from sections identical to this one, each layer representing a year of the ancient river's diminishing flows — a geological clock counting down to the moment the landscape became the Mirror Flats.
@@yThe section is unstable and requires careful footing — the exposed stratigraphy is beautiful as geological records go but fragile, each seasonal layer only as thick as a few fingers pressed together.@@N
