@@yMineral Seep@@N~
@@yA rare moisture point in the Mirror Flats supports a small cluster of mineral-stained ground where salt-tolerant organisms cluster around the seep's upwelling — a barely-visible spring that produces water too mineral-saturated for most purposes but sufficient to support a specialized micro-ecology.
@@bGlass scorpions and salt-adapted serpents hunt the seep's margins, drawn by the concentration of smaller organisms that the moisture supports — the predator density at this location is significantly higher than the surrounding Flats, and guides mark it as a fauna caution zone on their route maps.
@@yThe water itself is white with mineral content, pooling in a shallow depression before evaporating or re-absorbing into the crust — a brief hydrological cycle that has been running for as long as the Flats have existed, unrelated to any current rainfall or aquifer.@@N
