@@ySurveyor's Cairn Remnant@@N~
@@yThe Surveyor's Cairn Remnant is one of the better-preserved examples of the Keeper tide-obelisk survey network on the Mirror Flats — a squat basalt post with Littoral Branch glyphs still carved in three of its four faces, recording tidal measurement data, a cargo-draft specification, and a witness-seal acknowledgment from a Keeper survey season.
@@bThe fourth face has been abraded to near-illegibility by centuries of salt-wind — a reminder that the Littoral Branch inscription tradition was adapted for coastal conditions specifically because the inland Measure Script's sandstone medium did not survive maritime weathering.
@@yScholar guides who work the Flats use this cairn as a teaching example: the tidal glyph on the north face can still be read as encoding a third-quarter tide rise with a strong northerly wind — specific meteorological data preserved in carved basalt because the Keepers believed that every measurement worth taking was worth carving in stone.@@N
