@@yCargo Sled Ruts@@N~
@@yThe deepest cargo sled ruts on the entire Heavy Road are found here — multi-year accumulation of parallel grooves worn by the heaviest cargo sleds, some deep enough that a person could lie in them with the surface at shoulder height.
@@bThe ruts document the history of the route's traffic: the oldest grooves are the widest, cut by the broad-beam cargo sleds of the Ledger-Tariff era; the more recent grooves are narrower and shallower, reflecting the decline in heavy cargo traffic as the Fracture Era scattered the great caravan leagues.
@@yThe rut pattern shows several decades of drift in alignment as the road's authority markers were replaced by successors who set them at slightly different positions — the road gradually shifting north over time as each generation of markers influenced the next generation of traffic patterns.@@N
