#31225
@@yThird Assessment Station — Main Hall@@N~
@@yThe main assessment chamber is the most legally significant room in the Mirror Flats South crossing — the place where cargo's inland valuation under water-debt standards was converted to maritime valuation under draft-weight and storm-risk standards, the Shoreward Conversion's core transaction rendered physical in stone and bronze.
@@bThe walls carry the most complete surviving Shoreward Table inscription in the Reach: conversion ratio charts carved in the Legal-Conversion voice, showing the mathematical relationship between inland assessment units and maritime equivalents across multiple cargo categories.
@@yThe extra descriptions carved into the table surfaces still carry reading-accessible examples: forty-seven measures water-debt for bulk grain, converted to fourteen stone draft-weight at standard Mafdet harbor rate, storm class moderate, holding cost three days harbor, risk assumption to ship captain upon oath.
@@bThe assessment platform at the room's center is intact but stripped — the scales removed, the counterweight collection gone, only the platform itself surviving because it was built into the floor rather than bolted to it.
@@yA Warden inspection notice posted on the east wall designates this room as protected heritage under the Warden's transit-seal authority — a classification that no one disputes and no one enforces, but which has so far prevented the station's demolition for building materials.@@N
