@@yBrine Pool@@N~
@@yThe Brine Pool is a standing body of mineral-saturated water that has maintained itself through subsurface seeping for as long as the Mirror Flats have existed — a geological feature independent of seasonal weather, its level stable across years and decades because the seep rate matches the evaporation rate in a nearly perfect equilibrium.
@@bThe water is too mineral-saturated to drink safely without treatment, but it serves as a hydration source for the fauna that have adapted to its chemistry — including the brine-adapted ambush predators that use its banks as hunting territory and the glass scorpions that come to drink and to hunt the things that drink.
@@yThe pool's surface reflects the sky and the surrounding glass terrain in a different quality than the salt mirror — the brine water's surface tension creating a more perfect reflection, so that the pool seems to contain a complete second world below the surface.@@N
