@@yThermal Seep Fissure@@N~
@@yA crack in the amber glass crust here exposes the thermal fault beneath — not dramatically, not with flame or smoke, but with a visible shimmer of rising heat and a faint sulfurous mineral smell that identifies the fault's chemistry.
@@bThe fissure is finger-wide, running for perhaps three body-lengths in a roughly east-west orientation before both ends descend below the surface again. Looking into the crack reveals glass layers in cross-section — the amber zone above, then a transition layer of mixed color, then a darker formation below that appears to predate even the pre-catastrophe milky glass.
@@yThe ground immediately around the fissure is warm enough to deter the Salt Glass Scorpions that infest most of the Belt — the heat disrupts their thermoregulatory behavior. This makes the fissure's immediate vicinity safer from scorpion encounter than almost anywhere else in the amber zone, a counterintuitive fact that experienced Guides use as an impromptu campsite for clients during multi-day crossings.@@N
