@@yThermal Fault Ridge@@N~
@@yThe northern edge of the Belt rises here along a ridge of glass fused at higher temperature than the surrounding field — the Thermal Fault Ridge, where the subsurface heat channel runs close enough to the surface to have created distinctive amber-and-orange banded glass in a raised formation.
@@bFrom the ridge crest, the geometry of the Belt's glass zones is visible — the milky western approach, the amber central crossing, the violet southern field, and to the east, the thinning glass of the Eastern Shelf where the Belt's eastern margin begins to give way to the cairn district's mixed terrain. It is the most useful navigation vantage in the Belt, which is why Glasswalk Guides built the waystation within sight of it.
@@yThe ridge surface is newly fused glass in places — the thermal fault has been mildly active in recent seasons, producing sections of fresh orange-tinted glass that have not yet stabilized into the amber formation's firmer consistency. These unstable sections carry significant worm-transit risk, as the soft new glass provides poor structural support.@@N
