@@yWind-Cut Gallery@@N~
@@yThe Glass Belt's most visually striking formation occupies this chamber — wind erosion over centuries has cut the glass into flowing curved shapes, each one different, all following the logic of wind direction and particle impact in ways that produce organic, almost deliberate-looking forms.
@@bSome of the shapes have acquired names from Glasswalk Guides who used them as navigation references in their route songs — the Broken Sail (a curved amber plate that does resemble a billowed sail with a fracture line), the Warden's Seal (a nearly circular amber disc raised on a glass pedestal), the Scorpion's Claw (self-explanatory). Guide students are brought here to learn the named formations as a route-song vocabulary exercise.
@@yThe acoustic properties of the gallery are remarkable — sound in the curved glass space produces a filtering effect, suppressing certain frequencies and amplifying others, so speech sounds different here than anywhere else in the Belt. The Guides have incorporated this acoustic signature into their route songs as a checkpoint — a specific note that should sound one way in the open Belt and differently if you have correctly entered the Gallery.@@N
