@@ySubglass Worm Warren@@N~
@@yThe glass crust here gives way to an exposed section of the worm warren system — a large void chamber where multiple transit channels converge, open to the surface through a partial collapse that has since stabilized.
@@bThe warren interior is accessible — barely. The entry point requires a crawl-through section, after which the chamber opens enough to stand in. The interior walls are the compacted granular material of worm-passage debris, polished smooth by generations of worm transit, with occasional exposed glass formations at the chamber's upper extent where the crust overhead has not collapsed.
@@yThe chamber itself currently contains no worms — the warren's occupants are absent, presumably in transit elsewhere in their territory. The ambient smell of the warren is distinctive: the mineral-warm scent of compressed glass particles combined with an organic component that is the worm's own chemical signature, embedded in the warren walls by repeated passage.@@N
