@@yEastern Glass Shelf@@N~
@@yThe glass belt thins here to a final shelf of mixed milky-and-amber formation, the belt's eastern edge where centuries of wind action have gradually deposited glass fragments into the cairn district's sandy terrain rather than vice versa — the Belt slowly colonizing eastward.
@@bThe shelf is firmer than it appears — the mixed formation has unusual compressive strength from the interlocking of two glass types with different thermal histories. Glasswalk Guides use the shelf as a final orientation point: the route songs end here, with a verse that describes identifying the three cairn towers visible on the horizon and using their angular separation to determine approach bearing.
@@yFrom the shelf's slight elevation, the geography ahead is clear — the cairn district's ruined infrastructure extends eastward in a loose crescent pattern, the remnant towers and assessment station walls visible at various distances, the whole district arranged as if the shelf is the best seat in the house for understanding the cairn system's original spatial logic.@@N
