@@yGlass Shelf Overlook@@N~
@@yThe highest point on the Eastern Glass Shelf provides an unobstructed view east across the cairn district and south toward the Tidemouth Approach — a panorama that would have been valuable for any traveler trying to orient themselves after the disorientation of the Belt's interior.
@@bThe view reveals strategic information that ground-level transit does not: the cairn district's ruined stations form visible clusters separated by open terrain, the approach routes between clusters legible from elevation in a way that the ground-level maze of rubble walls obscures. The circuit from here to Measure's Rest, visible as the highest standing structure in the district, appears direct — ground transit is considerably less so.
@@yA flat surface near the overlook's highest point bears the scoring of a Glasswalk Guide's route-song final verse — the traditional closing notation that a Guide is supposed to add each time they complete a successful crossing with a client. This one has been added to many times, the overlapping scoring creating a dense text that can no longer be fully read without considerable patience.@@N
