@@yGuide's Song Marker@@N~
@@yA low basalt stone — not native to this terrain, transported here deliberately — stands at a junction in the terrain, its surface covered in the finger-width scoring of Glasswalk Guide route notation.
@@bThe stone marks the end of the route song for the Saltglass crossing — a concluding verse that names the three cairn cluster landmarks visible from the Eastern Shelf, gives the bearing to Measure's Rest, and ends with the standard Guide closing notation: transit complete, client delivered, tariff applies. The stone serves as the final waymark of the Guide service, the official termination point of their navigational responsibility.
@@yA secondary inscription on the stone's reverse side, in different scoring style and apparent date, notes that the crossing has been completed four hundred and sixty-seven times since the stone was placed — each completion noted by a single incision in a tally system that has been added to for decades. The tally marks are densest near the top where the stone was first scored, and progressively less dense toward the base as the counting system ran out of space and had to continue on lower sections.@@N
