@@yCairn Line Approach@@N~
@@yThe first cairn station is visible from here — not the intact structure it once was, but enough of its original form survives to be recognizable: a stone base course three courses high at its most intact corner, the outline of the assessment floor still legible in the foundation geometry, a partial wind-baffle wall standing at an angle that suggests the prevailing wind direction the original builders planned against.
@@bThis was the most western of the district's outer stations, the first inspection point encountered by travelers arriving from the Mirror Flats. Its position required it to process the highest volume of transit traffic — the Heavy Road's broad cargo loads as well as the lighter Swift Line freight — which is why its foundation footprint is considerably larger than the eastern stations more specialized for single-route cargo.
@@yThe ruins show a particular pattern of damage: the bronze fittings that anchored the weighing platforms and document cabinets were removed cleanly and early, their mounting holes still visible in the stone. The Cairn Scavengers who stripped this station were methodical rather than destructive — they wanted the metal, not the ruin.@@N
