@@yStation One: Bronze Gate@@N~
@@yThe cairn station that once marked this position was named for its characteristic bronze gate — a double door of worked bronze that served as the physical representation of the assessment system's authority. The gate itself is long gone, stripped to the bare stone mounting posts, but the posts survive and the scale of the original gate can be reconstructed from the spacing and hinge-pocket dimensions.
@@bThe gate would have been imposing — the pocket dimensions indicate a door panel over two body-heights tall, the bronze thickness at the hinge points suggesting a weight that required a counterbalance mechanism to operate. The station's stripped interior floor shows the anchor points for the counterbalance housing, another target for the Scavengers who preferred the brass counterbalance weights to the structural bronze.
@@yScavengers are present here now — two figures working at the station's northwest corner, methodically removing stone from a collapsed section that appears to conceal something in the rubble below. Their tools are purpose-made for ruin excavation: narrow pry bars calibrated for stone work, collection bags sized for specific fittings.@@N
