@@yStation Three: Assessment Court@@N~
@@yStation Three was the district's primary Shoreward Assessor facility — the station where the assessment process was actually conducted rather than merely logged, and as a result it was built to a higher specification than the other outer stations. The wind baffles survive here, angled stone projections designed to reduce wind turbulence during document work.
@@bThe Assessment Court's layout reflects the assessment process's sequence: the receiving bay (south wall), the weighing platform area (center, foundations still visible), the document preparation tables (east wall, stone surfaces with ink-staining still faintly visible), and the Assessor's verification station (north corner, elevated slightly above the court floor to give the Assessor a view of all operations simultaneously).
@@yThe Shoreward Assessors were not Wardens — they were a separate guild that contracted to the Littoral Branch, specializing in the conversion of inland cargo value into the maritime legal terminology required for shore transit. Their presence in the cairn district was the point where the land-administrative system formally reached out to maritime law, the assessment here being the moment of jurisdictional handshake.@@N
