@@yCrossroads Approach@@N~
@@yThe approach to the Cairn Station Crossroads — the hub where all three route systems converged — is marked by a surviving section of the original approach avenue: matching stone border markers on both sides of the path, each inscribed with the triple-route notation that indicated this was a compound assessment zone.
@@bThe triple-route notation is significant in the Littoral Branch system — it meant that cargo arriving with any of the three certifications was legally equivalent at this point, which removed the administrative premium that the Swift Line's speed and the Sealed Route's security normally commanded. At the Crossroads, a bale of trade goods was a bale of trade goods regardless of how it arrived.
@@yA south branch leads to a guardhouse remnant — the defensive position that protected the approach to the Crossroads from unauthorized transit, a reminder that the compound assessment zone required enforcement as well as administration.@@N
