@@yMeasure's Rest: Sealed Archive@@N~
@@yThe sealed archive below Measure's Rest contains the most sensitive and valuable tablets in the district — not the routine transit records of the Library Hall above, but the legal instruments that established the assessment system's jurisdictional authority: original charter documents, precedent-setting case records, and the Shore Conversion tables in their most complete surviving form.
@@bThe archive is cold compared to the desert above — the underground chamber maintains a lower temperature that the Wardens and Tidewright scholars both recognize as beneficial for tablet preservation, a thermal buffering effect of the surrounding stone mass. The tablets here are correspondingly better preserved than those in the Library Hall above.
@@yThe most significant object in the archive is the Shoreward Compact Original — the founding document of the three-route assessment system, inscribed on a triple-panel basalt tablet whose survival through the Fracture Era seems to have been a matter of the archive's construction rather than deliberate curation. The Compact defines the jurisdictional handshake between inland and maritime law that every shore conversion in the Reach's history derived from.@@N
