#31342
@@yOld Sealed Route Junction@@N~
@@yThe Old Sealed Route Junction has a different character from the other outer junctions — its construction included internal walls and sealed storage chambers not present in the other facilities, designed for the special handling requirements of the funerary and relic consignments that the Sealed Route specialized in.
@@bThe sealed chambers are still sealed — not locked with mechanisms that time or scavenging have broken, but sealed with construction: the original mortar reinforced with a bitumen compound that was intended to be permanent rather than removable. Whatever the chambers contain has never been removed, and the Wardens' current policy is to leave them sealed until scholarly consensus on how to approach the contents is reached.
@@yQuarantine markers surround the junction — the distinctive three-bar notation of the Littoral Branch's Hazard Reclassification system, indicating that materials at this station were assessed as hazardous at some point in the system's operation. Whether the hazard notation refers to the chambers' current contents or to cargo that passed through in transit cannot be determined without accessing the records, and the records are in the sealed chambers.@@N
