@@yEastern Cairn Line — Western Section@@N~
@@yThe Eastern Cairn Line represents the final surviving stretch of the original route system's intact marker infrastructure — cairns at regular intervals, their stones not stripped, the capstone orientations still correct, the distance inscriptions still legible. It survived because it was too far from the market activity to be economically worth stripping.
@@bThe intact cairns here provide the clearest surviving example of what the full Reach's marker infrastructure looked like before the Fracture Era's abandonment began the stripping process. Each cairn is the standard design: foundation course, assessment-height body, capstone with four-face inscription, shadow-rod socket centered in the capstone's upper surface.
@@yThe transit corridor between the cairns is also the best-preserved in the district — the drainage channels are functional, the road surface compacted to the original specification, and even the shoulder markers that indicated the assessment inspection zone boundary are still in place. The last kilometer of the Littoral Branch's working infrastructure, preserved by remoteness.@@N
