@@yEastern Cairn Line — Terminal Marker@@N~
@@yThe final cairn marker of the Saltglass Reach's inland route system stands here — the last stone in a chain of assessment markers that extended from the Tidemouth to the Mirror Flats' western edge, each one a physical representation of the legal territory between interior commerce and maritime law.
@@bThe terminal marker is inscribed on all four capstone faces with the Shore Conversion jurisdictional declaration — the formal statement that beyond this point, inland transit certification is converted to maritime documentation under the Shoreward Table system, and inland legal instruments yield to maritime law. It is both a navigation marker and a legal pronouncement.
@@yThe marker's inscription is the most worn of any on the Eastern Cairn Line, the result of being the most often-read stone in the district — every traveler who reached this point and understood what the inscription meant would stop to read it, and the cumulative touch of generations of reading fingers has smoothed the inscription's depth more than centuries of weather.@@N
