#31350
@@ySealedRoute Marker One@@N~
@@yThe Sealed Route's first stone marker survives intact — a broad basalt slab set upright at the road margin, its four carved faces each bearing a different category of quarantine warning in the Littoral Branch's Hazard Reclassification notation.
@@bThe four warning categories inscribed here represent the full scope of the Sealed Route's special mandate: consignment origin verification (did the relic come from a legitimately listed source), content classification (was it properly identified under the current hazard framework), handling restriction (what specific protocols applied during transit), and disposition authority (who could authorize release at the Tidemouth end).
@@yThis four-way classification was the Sealed Route's distinguishing characteristic — the transit system designed for objects that the standard assessment process could not handle, because the objects required more information about their origin and nature than cargo manifests normally captured.
@@bThe route's abandonment during the Fracture Era left these markers as the most complete surviving record of the hazard classification system they served. Scholars who want to understand how the Littoral Branch handled relic traffic must work primarily from these marker inscriptions, the administrative records having been dispersed during the Fracture.
@@yA quarantine boundary marker is still planted beside the basalt slab — a wooden post with the three-bar quarantine notation in iron, rusted but legible, indicating that the Sealed Route's legal classification as a quarantine transit zone remains formally in effect. Nobody has officially canceled the quarantine designation, since the legal process for doing so requires the authority of the Littoral Branch office that no longer exists.@@N
