#31400
@@yFirst Crab Burrow@@N~
@@yThe First Crab Burrow is the coastal zone's most recognized landmark for travelers approaching from the interior — a complex of Tidemouth Crab burrow openings arranged in a loose arc that marks the point where coastal ecology definitively replaces desert ecology, and where, by tradition, overland travelers stop to acknowledge that the Reach proper is behind them and the shore conversion zone is ahead.
@@bThe burrow complex is active — Tidemouth Crabs are visible at the openings, the characteristic pale-shelled coastal crabs that dig burrows to the water table and collapse the surrounding ground when disturbed. A tradition among Sand-Sea Carters holds that counting the number of visible crabs at the First Burrow predicts the tide state at Mafdet Harbor — an empirically unreliable method that has been in use long enough to acquire the authority of tradition.
@@yThe landmark inscription stands nearby — a basalt block carved by the Sand-Sea Carters' Guild with the traditional half-day notation: from this point to Mafdet Harbor is one half-day at a loaded cart's pace in favorable conditions, one full day in fog or heavy dune drift, two days in the worst of the Fog Season when the coastal path becomes navigable only by guide-rope between established posts.
@@bThe Crab Burrow landmark is also where the overlapping jurisdictions begin to matter practically: the Sealed Route approach from the west, the Cairn District descent from the north, and the coastal approach from the east all converge here, and none of the three governing legal frameworks is unambiguously applicable to the First Burrow zone itself.
@@yThe burrow complex's central depression — where multiple burrows intersect underground — creates a collapse risk zone approximately three body-lengths across. The soft ground at the center is visually distinguishable from the firm surrounding terrain by its slightly different color and the faint ringing hollow sound it produces when tapped. Experienced coastal travelers avoid the center; newcomers often do not receive this advice in time.@@N
