@@bThe burrow density reflects the underlying water table: the crabs dig to water, and where the water table is closest to the surface they can dig shallower burrows more efficiently, supporting higher population densities. The First Burrow zone sits directly over a subsurface water channel that feeds the brackish table of the entire coastal approach.
@@yTide Crab carapace fragments are scattered across the surface — the crabs molt when they grow, and the molted carapaces dry and fragment in the coastal wind, creating a perpetual light dusting of carapace material that gives the terrain a distinctive textured appearance when the light is right.@@N
