@@yTidal Drainage Channel@@N~
@@yA tidal drainage channel crosses the approach path here — a shallow depression that carries water during high tide and stands dry between tidal cycles. The channel's banks are marked by a distinct band of salt-tolerant vegetation, the only plant life in the otherwise exposed coastal terrain.
@@bThe vegetation band is useful for navigation — the plants mark the channel's course across the open terrain where the channel itself is not always visible, providing a linear reference feature in a landscape that otherwise offers few landmarks for cross-terrain navigation.
@@yThe channel is crossable at its shallowest point during low tide — a ford marked by the traditional flat stone that a Carter has maintained in the crossing position for as long as anyone can reliably remember. At high tide the crossing requires wading; in storm conditions it is impassable without a guide rope.@@N
