@@yBrackish Channel Crossing@@N~
@@yThe Brackish Channel is wider and deeper than the tidal drainage channels — a permanent brackish water feature that marks one of the Tidemouth Approach's most significant terrain boundaries, the boundary between the drier coastal flat zone to the west and the salt marsh proper to the east.
@@bThe water in the channel is brackish rather than salt — a mixture of the tidal saltwater that backs up into it during high tide and the mineral-laden groundwater that feeds it continuously, producing a water chemistry that supports a distinctive ecology of salt-tolerant but not salt-dependent organisms.
@@yA rough ford provides crossing at low tide — flat stones placed for foot crossings, with a wooden stake marker showing the water depth at the ford's deepest point graduated in hand-widths. At the current mark the crossing is knee-deep, which the carter at the ford indicates is slightly above average for the current tidal cycle.@@N
