@@ySaltmarsh Entry@@N~
@@yEast of the Brackish Channel the terrain becomes genuinely marshy — the ground surface is yielding, the underlying salt clay holding moisture from the tidal cycles, and the plant life changes from the sparse coastal desert vegetation to the dense, low-growing saltmarsh species that characterize the Tidemouth Approach.
@@bThe saltmarsh vegetation is well-adapted to the alternate wet-dry cycle of the tidal approach: low-growing to minimize wind exposure during storms, root systems extensive to anchor in the clay-saturated soil during tidal water movement, and salt-excreting mechanisms that visibly coat the leaves with crystalline salt residue in warm conditions.
@@yThe scent here is distinctly coastal — salt and organic marsh, quite different from the mineral-dominant smell of the desert and glass belt zones that characterize the Reach's interior. For travelers who have spent time in the interior, the scent is an abrupt sensory transition.@@N
