@@yTidemouth Flats@@N~
@@yThe Tidemouth Flats are the widest section of the coastal approach — a broad expanse of level salt-clay terrain that extends to the north and south further than the eye can easily follow, the flatness broken only by the irregular mounding of Tidemouth Crab colony complexes and the occasional standing stone of a Keeper-era tidal marker.
@@bThe Flats are the most hazardous terrain in the coastal approach due to the concentration of Tidemouth Crab activity — the flat terrain provides ideal burrowing conditions, the water table is accessible at a consistent depth across the entire area, and the tidal cycle provides the wet-dry alternation that the crab population requires for its feeding and reproduction patterns.
@@yThe collapse risk from the crab burrows is higher on the Flats than anywhere else in the coastal zone — the burrow density creates a subsurface void network extensive enough that significant sections of the surface are structurally compromised. Traditional crossing routes through the Flats are established by the Sand-Sea Carters' Guild based on annual survey mapping of the burrow complex, and deviating from the established routes significantly increases collapse risk.
@@bAt low tide, the Flats reveal the tidal network that feeds the crab population — shallow channels in the salt clay that carry water from the sea at high tide and drain completely at low tide, their presence only visible in the different coloration of the clay along the channel margins.
@@yThe Flats smell of brine, salt clay, and the organic compound that the Tidemouth Crabs excrete during their burrowing activity — a complex coastal scent that experienced coastal travelers associate specifically with this zone and that serves as an olfactory warning to new arrivals that they are in the most heavily crab-worked terrain of the approach.@@N
