@@yTidal Pool Grotto@@N~
@@yThe Tidal Pool Grotto is a coastal cave formed by wave action in the rock shelf — a sheltered space that is half-exposed at low tide and fully underwater at high tide, the alternation supporting a particularly rich tidal-zone ecology that has attracted the attention of natural historians from both the Reach and Mafdet's maritime community.
@@bThe grotto's walls are covered with attached marine organisms — filter-feeders that tolerate the exposure and submersion cycle, their color and texture creating a living fresco that changes appearance between high and low tide as different organisms' feeding behaviors become visible or dormant.
@@yAmphibious fauna associated with the grotto include a species of large coastal frog whose tolerance for brackish water makes it unusual among amphibians, and a semi-aquatic lizard that has adapted to hunting in the tidal zone's edge habitats. Both species are known to natural historians but poorly documented, and the grotto is one of the few sites where both can be reliably observed.@@N
