@@yCrab Warren@@N~
@@yThe Crab Warren is the largest Tidemouth Crab colony in the coastal approach — a complex that covers significant ground area and extends deep enough underground to reach the permanent water table rather than just the brackish tidal table that the shallower colonies depend on.
@@bThe Warren's depth means its structure is more stable than the shallower burrow complexes — the deeper gallery system is supported by the bedrock layer that limits the Warren's downward extent, and the lateral galleries are reinforced by the accumulated calcium carbonate from generations of crab shell material incorporated into the gallery walls.
@@yDespite the greater structural stability of the deep Warren, the surface over the Warren's largest voids is still subject to collapse under sufficient load — the reinforced galleries are deep enough that direct collapse is unlikely, but the horizontal extent of the void network means that load applied on the surface can cause lateral stress that triggers gallery failure at points some distance from the load position.@@N
