@@yMineral-Stained Dune@@N~
@@yThe Mineral-Stained Dune is a vivid interruption in the coastal approach's muted palette — a dune whose sand has been colored by the coastal mineral deposits from the underlying rock layer exposed by tidal erosion, creating bands of red, orange, and yellow in the sand face that contrast sharply with the pale salt-grey of the surrounding terrain.
@@bThe coloration is iron oxide and sulfur compound staining from a mineral seep at the dune's base — the seep saturates the sand and the mineral compounds precipitate as the water evaporates, creating the color bands visible from considerable distance. The Carters use the dune as a landmark, visible in clear conditions from the First Crab Burrow in the west and from Mafdet Harbor's northern approach road in the east.
@@yThe mineral compounds that color the dune are not hazardous but are the same compounds that characterize the brine from the coastal wells — the deep brackish table that feeds the coastal zone's water system carries these minerals throughout the approach zone, making the Mineral-Stained Dune a concentrated visible expression of a chemistry that pervades the entire coastal hydrology.@@N
