@@bThe openness has practical consequences: navigation here requires the established route stakes because there are no landmark ruins or geological features to provide orientation. A traveler who loses the stakes in fog — the Fog Season is notorious for reducing visibility to arm's length — can become disoriented quickly in terrain that provides no visual reference beyond the stake system.
@@yThe wind here is persistently from the sea — the coastal onshore wind that carries the salt fog in appropriate conditions and the smell of the sea continuously. Experienced coastal travelers can read the wind's character for information about approaching weather conditions, the smell and humidity varying in ways that predict fog formation timing.@@N
