#31425
@@ySaltmarsh Trail@@N~
@@yThe Saltmarsh Trail winds through the coastal vegetation in a route that a generation of carter route-finding has established as the optimal path through the most densely vegetated section of the approach — the route that maximizes firm footing and minimizes vegetation resistance while maintaining a generally eastward direction.
@@bThe trail's winding character reflects the distribution of the saltmarsh plants' root masses — the dense root systems of the mature specimens are too difficult to push through, and the trail finds the gaps between them. From above, the trail's path would read as a record of the vegetation's growth pattern over the decades that the trail has been in use.
@@yThe vegetation here is taller than in the outer marsh zone — the plants have been growing undisturbed at this distance from the high-traffic outer approach, and the specimens here are mature and dense. The trail through them creates an enclosed feeling quite different from the open plain of the mid-approach.@@N
