@@yResin Dump Approach@@N~
@@yThe smell of the Resin Dump Clearing arrives before the visual evidence — the pine-tar and mineral-resin compounds used in hull caulking have a distinctive, penetrating smell that carries downwind for some distance, and the dump's long accumulation of these compounds has created a persistent olfactory signature in the coastal wind.
@@bThe resin dump is evidence of the Tidemouth Approach's historic role in Mafdet's ship maintenance cycle — where ships' hull maintenance was conducted before and after harbor entry, the approach zone serving as a working maritime-industrial area before it transitioned to its current primarily transit function.
@@yThe dump's age is visible in the weathering state of the accumulated material — the most recent deposits are still sticky in warm weather, the older deposits are dry and cracked, and the oldest are fully desiccated mineral residue that has lost all the organic resin component and retains only the mineral fillers.@@N
