#31433
@@yBrackish Well@@N~
@@yThe Brackish Well is the last reliable freshwater source on the approach to Mafdet Harbor — the water is mineral-tainted and slightly salty, but drinkable and replenishing in a way that the fully salt coastal water is not. Its position in the contested jurisdiction zone makes it strategically significant in ways that a simple water source would not normally command.
@@bThe well is ancient — the stone casing dates to the Keeper period based on the construction style, and the depth suggests access to a water table at the limit of practical hand-digging. The water quality varies with the tidal cycle, becoming more saline at high tide as the salt table rises and less saline at low tide, the diurnal variation requiring travelers to draw water at low tide for the best drinking quality.
@@yThe well is administered by whoever currently controls the jurisdiction claim for the surrounding zone, which changes with the contested authority situation. The current administration is the Tidemouth Warden post, which charges a regulated access fee that it remits to a disputed account held by both Warden organizations pending jurisdictional resolution.@@N
