@@yThe pre-fog zone is identifiable in certain weather conditions by a specific quality of light and air — not fog itself yet, but the conditions that precede the coastal fog that characterizes the Tidemouth Approach in the Fog Season and in morning hours throughout most of the year.
@@bThe precursor conditions include a particular change in the ambient moisture level that is detectable on skin and in the changed acoustic environment — sound travels differently in the moisture-saturated air, voices carry further but lose definition, and certain frequencies are attenuated while others seem amplified.
@@yThis zone is where travelers typically make the decision whether to proceed or to camp and wait for fog clearance — the pre-fog conditions make the actual fog imminent enough that a decision made here is still meaningful. Beyond this point, the fog zone itself may have already established before the decision becomes necessary.@@N
