@@yAssessment Hall of the Swift Line@@N~
@@yThe Swift Line Assessment Hall is the northernmost of the three terminal conversion facilities — a structure whose surviving elements reflect the Swift Line's operational character: the hall is smaller than the Heavy Road facility, built for faster throughput rather than bulk-cargo capacity.
@@bThe Swift Line conversion process differed from the central route: pre-certified cargo from trusted carriers received abbreviated assessment, the certification verification focused on the carrier's authorization rather than the cargo itself. This allowed the Swift Line to maintain its speed premium all the way to the Tidemouth conversion, not just through the interior crossing.
@@yThe Hall's document processing areas show the pre-certification system's physical requirements: verification stations for the carrier's authorization medallion (the bronze fitting that was both credential and key in the Swift Line's certification system), the inscription surfaces where the maritime bill of lading was formally endorsed, and the seal application station where the conversion was made legally binding.@@N
