@@ySwift Line Hall — Interior@@N~
@@yThe interior of the Swift Line Assessment Hall houses the most complete surviving conversion documentation system in the Shoreward complex — the stone surfaces where maritime bills of lading were prepared retain legible text from hundreds of completed conversions, the inscription system having been conducted directly into the stone rather than onto tablets.
@@bThe inscribed conversions record the Swift Line's range of cargo — the transit types that the pre-certification system trusted enough to expedite. The cargo categories are not uniformly mundane: several conversions are for cargo described in the Measure Script category that the Tidewright scholars have translated as "strategic materials," a category whose precise definition is not clear from the surviving records.
@@yThe most recent conversion inscriptions — those made during the system's last operational period — show evidence of hurried work, the inscription quality deteriorating from the careful standard of the earlier records. The last conversion inscribed here appears to have been conducted under circumstances that did not permit the normal careful procedure.@@N
