#31455
@@yAssessment Hall of the Heavy Road@@N~
@@yThe Assessment Hall of the Heavy Road is the largest of the three terminal conversion facilities — its scale reflects the Heavy Road's volume: bulk cargo from the Mirror Flats' most heavily trafficked route required a hall capable of handling simultaneous assessments of multiple large cargo consignments, the assessment process necessarily more complex for mixed bulk cargoes than for the Swift Line's pre-certified single-carrier loads.
@@bThe Hall's surviving structure includes the most elaborate assessment infrastructure remaining in the Shoreward complex: multiple weighing platforms (the foundation courses and anchor holes visible for at least five distinct platforms), a specialized water measurement system for liquid commodities (the basin and channel system partially intact), and the extended document preparation area for the complex manifests that bulk cargo required.
@@yThe conversion documentation produced here was the most legally dense of the three route types — each bulk cargo assessment required individual item documentation, cargo class assignments, risk assessments for mixed-type loads, and the complex Shore Conversion calculation that translated inland weight-based value into the maritime volume-and-risk format.
@@bThe Hall shows evidence of the most intensive historical use of the three facilities: the weighing platform surfaces are worn deeper than in the Swift Line Hall, the document preparation surfaces have more accumulated inscription layers, and the maintenance records (visible as patches and repairs in the original stonework) indicate significantly more wear over the operational period.
@@yThe Heavy Road Hall is the best surviving example of the Shore Conversion system's most complete operational form — the assessment infrastructure for the Reach's primary cargo route preserved in more complete form than anywhere else in the complex, its scale implying the commercial volumes that made the Saltglass Reach administratively significant rather than merely geographically interesting.@@N
