@@bThe annexe equipment shows the extended-review process's requirements: a writing desk with a specific-purpose notation system for disputed cargo classification cases, reference materials for the cargo type categories that most commonly required extended review (mixed-origin loads, goods with incomplete provenance documentation, commodities whose classification changed between inland assessment and shore assessment), and the records of completed extended reviews.
@@yThe extended-review records preserved here represent the Shore Conversion system's most interesting administrative history — the cases where the inland-to-maritime classification system broke down in specific ways, the solutions that the Assessors developed case-by-case, and the precedents that were formally noted for future reference.@@N
