#31465
@@yWater-Debt Discharge Basin@@N~
@@yThe Water-Debt Discharge Basin is the Shore Conversion's most symbolic facility — a large basalt basin where the water-debt component of inland transit was formally terminated before the cargo's legal transformation to maritime documentation.
@@bThe water-debt system was one of the Littoral Branch's most distinctive economic instruments: inland transit incurred a water-debt obligation calculated from the water used by pack animals, cooking, and washing during the crossing, payable at the Tidemouth in maritime terms. The Basin was where this obligation was symbolically discharged — cargo masters poured water (from the Brackish Well, traditionally) into the basin while reciting the debt-termination formula, and the Assessor certified the discharge in the conversion documentation.
@@yThe Basin is full of sand now — centuries of wind-blown accumulation have partially filled it. But the overflow channels carved in the basin rim are still functional, and the carved inscription on the basin's exterior that records the debt-termination formula is legible in full. The formula is considerably longer than would seem necessary for a practical transaction, suggesting it had ritual as well as administrative functions.@@N
