@@yBasin Water Source Chamber@@N~
@@yThe chamber above the Water-Debt Basin stored the water used in the debt-termination ritual — water carried from the Brackish Well and held here until the formal ceremony required it. The chamber's stone storage vessels are still in place, their interiors showing the mineral staining of long use with the brackish coastal water.
@@bThe storage arrangement reflects the ritual's procedural requirements: specific volumes of water, maintained in specific vessels, added to the Basin in a specific sequence during the termination ceremony. The sequence was ceremonially significant — the wrong sequence invalidated the ceremony and required the entire procedure to be repeated.
@@yA secondary inscription in the chamber records the water volume calculations for different debt amounts — the table that converted inland transit water-usage estimates into the specific volume of water required for the termination ceremony. The conversion between water used and water discharged was not one-to-one but followed a calculation that factored in the debt's duration and the crossing conditions.@@N
