@@yRisk Assumption Oath Court@@N~
@@yThe Risk Assumption Oath Court is the most formally designed space in the Shoreward complex — a chamber built specifically for the oath procedure, with the oath-taker's position marked in inset basalt, the Assessor's station elevated for authority over the proceeding, and witness positions arranged to ensure unobstructed sight lines for all participants.
@@bThe oath inscribed in the court's dedication text transfers liability through the maritime concept of assumed risk — the cargo master taking the oath accepted that from this point, the cargo's fate was in the maritime framework's hands, and the inland transit system's obligations were satisfied. The maritime insurance system at Mafdet then covered the cargo from the Shoreward Seal forward.
@@yThe most unusual feature of the Oath Court is the secondary oath inscription below the main text — a parallel oath in a script that is not the standard assessment notation but the maritime system's own legal language, suggesting the oath had to be taken in both legal frameworks simultaneously for full validity. The parallel text appears to be a direct translation of the primary oath, but several terms have no exact equivalent and appear in both original languages.@@N
