#31475
@@yThe Shoreward Seal@@N~
@@yThe Shoreward Seal is the area's central artifact — a great basalt slab three body-heights tall and one body-width thick, its four faces carved with the foundational legal text of the Shore Conversion system: the Shoreward Compact itself, in the original Measure Script-maritime hybrid notation that the two legal systems agreed to use for their shared instrument.
@@bThe Compact's text is carved in a quality of workmanship that no other inscription in the Reach matches — deep, precise, with the individual letters shaped by a master's chisel work rather than a practical scribe's expedient cutting. The effort invested in the inscription reflects the document's significance: this was not a working administrative text but the foundational legal instrument that everything else derived from.
@@yThe Seal's west face carries the inland text of the Compact, establishing the inland legal authority for the Shore Conversion system from the Littoral Branch framework. The east face carries the maritime text in the Harbor Charter's legal language. The north and south faces carry the mutual recognition clauses — the text where each system formally acknowledged the validity of the other, the clauses that made the conversion process legally binding in both jurisdictions simultaneously.
@@bThe Seal's current condition is excellent by the standards of the surrounding ruins — the maintenance effort of the Harbor Wardens and Tidewright scholars has prevented the surface degradation that affects every other inscription in the complex. The text is legible in full on all four faces.
@@yThe area immediately around the Seal shows the evidence of continuous attention: a formal visitor notation system (a clay tablet register where significant visitors inscribe their name and purpose), a maintenance supply cache visible behind the northern edge, and the worn marks of regular cleaning work on the paving stones closest to the Seal's base. The Seal has been visited, studied, and cared for throughout the period of the Reach's abandonment, a continuous chain of attention that is the most remarkable human achievement in the entire district.@@N
