#31482
@@yJurisdictional Threshold@@N~
@@yThe Jurisdictional Threshold is the exact legal boundary of the Shore Conversion system — the point identified in the Shoreward Compact as the geographic location where inland law formally yields to maritime jurisdiction for cargo certified under the conversion system.
@@bThe Threshold is marked by a dual-text inscription in the floor rather than a wall or standing stone — the legal boundary is meant to be crossed rather than approached, and the floor inscription requires walking over it. The inland authority text is inscribed on the western half of the floor panel; the maritime authority text on the eastern half; the exact center line is marked with a continuous incised line.
@@yLegally, the Shoreward Compact specifies that crossing the Threshold with properly converted documentation constitutes acceptance of maritime jurisdiction for the certified cargo — an act that historically was reversible only by returning through the Threshold and surrendering the maritime documentation. The practical irreversibility of crossing was part of the system's enforcement mechanism.@@N
