#31494
@@yToll-Marshal of the Three Routes@@N~
@@yThe Toll-Marshal of the Three Routes is the most powerful manifestation of the mechanism's jurisdictional conflict — an entity formed from the accumulated unresolved toll claims of all three route systems, embodying the administrative authority that the Compact's conversion process was supposed to transfer but instead left suspended in the frozen mechanism.
@@bThe Marshal is the size of a large person but composed differently — the jurisdictional conflicts that formed it are visible as competing material qualities in its physical composition, sections that appear to follow inland physical law alternating with sections that follow maritime physical law, the boundary between the two in constant, slow negotiation.
@@yThe Marshal's aggression is not territorial but administrative — it attempts to enforce toll collection against all who enter its chamber, demanding the triple-route certification that has not been legally issuable since the Compact's suspension. Its methods are not the methods of a creature but of a bureaucracy: it invokes authorities, demands documentation, and only becomes physical when its administrative challenge is refused.
@@bThose who have faced the Marshal and survived report that the most effective approach is neither combat nor compliance but the third option that the Compact's emergency procedures allowed — a procedural appeal to the mechanism itself, bypassing the Marshal's authority by invoking the foundational jurisdictional claim that predates any of the three routes.
@@yThe chamber walls bear the accumulated toll demands that the Marshal has issued to entities who did not survive the encounter — inscriptions in all three route certification systems, the demands growing more elaborate as the Marshal's isolation extended and its bureaucratic creativity operated without external feedback or constraint.@@N
