@@ySanctum Approach@@N~
@@yThe approach to the Sanctum of Contested Jurisdiction passes through a zone where the mechanism's conflict field reaches near-maximum intensity — the electromagnetic charge here causes continuous visible effects on metal and occasionally on organic materials, a constant shimmer of jurisdictional energy that makes the approach uncomfortable to traverse.
@@bThe discomfort is not merely physical — the conflict field produces cognitive effects at this intensity, a persistent sense of being in two places simultaneously, of having obligations to competing authorities that cannot be simultaneously satisfied. The Tidewright scholars who have theorized about this zone suggest the effect is the mechanism's conflict field operating on conscious minds the same way it operates on the physical mechanism elements.
@@yThe Sanctum entrance ahead is marked by the only inscription in the Undercroft that is written in all four known notation systems simultaneously — the same text in Measure Script, maritime notation, pre-Keeper proto-notation, and the unidentified fourth script, suggesting the Sanctum itself is beyond the jurisdiction of any single framework.@@N
