@@ySanctum of Contested Jurisdiction@@N~
@@yThe Sanctum of Contested Jurisdiction is the mechanism's second output chamber — the space where the competing frameworks are not merely in conflict but in active, ongoing legal contest, each asserting dominance over every physical and legal element in the chamber simultaneously.
@@bThe Sanctum's current occupant is the physical manifestation of this contest — an entity that cannot be definitively categorized in either the inland or maritime framework, that has legal reality in both simultaneously, and that enforces its authority over the Sanctum's space by asserting claims from both systems at once, requiring anyone in the chamber to either satisfy both jurisdictions' requirements or accept the consequences of being in violation of both.
@@yThe chamber shows evidence that the contest has been going on for an extremely long time — the competing inscriptions on the walls have been written, overwritten, challenged with counter-inscriptions, and the counter-inscriptions themselves overwritten in a layered record of jurisdictional dispute that approaches geological accumulation in its density.@@N
