@@yHeart Anteroom@@N~
@@yThe anteroom before the Heart of the Conversion is the quietest space in the Undercroft — the electromagnetic charge that pervades the other chambers is absent here, replaced by a stillness that feels charged with potential rather than active conflict.
@@bThe stillness is apparently a property of the mechanism's structure: this anteroom sits at the mechanism's fulcrum point, where the competing jurisdictional claims exactly balance each other, producing not resolution but cancellation — neither framework dominant, both frameworks present, their conflict momentarily suspended.
@@yThe floor inscription here is the only place in the Undercroft where the unidentified fourth notation system appears without any other notation alongside it — a pure text in the pre-human script, without the cross-referencing and translation attempts that appear in the other chambers. The text has not been decoded, but the scholars who have studied it note that it does not feel like a legal document. It feels like a memory.@@N
