A heavy-bodied serpent glides through the saltmarsh with the unhurried confidence of something near the top of the local food chain.
~
The saltmarsh serpent is larger than its Flats-dwelling relatives, taking advantage of the Tidemouth's richer prey population to grow to a size that inland salt vipers never reach. Its coloration matches the saltmarsh vegetation with the passive accuracy of long evolutionary adaptation, and its venom has shifted in its coastal environment to a formula optimized for the prey types available here — a different mixture from the Flats viper's, but equally effective at incapacitation. It moves between the tidal margin and the dune approach zone freely, as comfortable in the shallow brackish channels as on dry ground.
~
