A mass of armored coastal insects surges across the dune face in a pattern that suggests coordinated hunger rather than random movement.
~
The dune scarab swarm is drawn to the organic matter concentrated at the Tidemouth's approach zone — the detritus of both land-based transit operations and the high-tide deposits that the sea leaves on the dune base. Individually harmless, the scarabs in swarm configuration can strip exposed organic material at remarkable speed, and their coordinated threat response to larger threats produces a collectively effective defense from the swarm's component insects. The scarabs' armored carapaces produce a dry rattling sound as the swarm moves that is distinctive and serves as effective early warning to experienced coastal travelers.
~
